/*!
\page qtbridges-csharp-debugging.html
\title Debugging the generated code
\keyword debugging generated code

The following applies only to example / demo / test projects in this solution.
Let \b Foobar be one such project.

\section1 Build a project and debug generated code

To debug \b Foobar's generated code:

\list 1
\li Build project \b Foobar in the `Debug` configuration.

    \list
    \li The generated native solution will include a custom build step to copy
        the target binary \c{foo_bar.exe} to the \c{bin} directory, and also to
        write debug configuration properties pointing to that copied binary.
    \endlist

\li Open the generated native solution in another instance of Visual Studio.

    \list
    \li For example: right-click on \b Foobar, select
        \uicontrol "Open in Terminal" and run the following:

        \badcode
        devenv .\obj\Debug\net8.0\qtdotnet\native\build\foo_bar.sln
        \endcode

    \li Now you have two Visual Studio instances running side-by-side:
        \list
        \li \b VS#1 with the \e qtbridge-csharp.sln solution
        \li \b VS#2 with the \e foo_bar.sln solution
        \endlist
    \endlist

\li Switch to \b VS#2 and press F5.

    \list
    \li The \b Foobar app starts in debug mode.
    \li It should be possible to break anywhere in the app's code.
    \endlist
\endlist

\note By default, the "mixed mode" debugger is selected. This allows breaking
and stepping through both the native and managed code of the app. There is
however, a limitation to using this debugger mode, namely that it will only be
possible to step into/through native code once the \e managed debug session is
running. In practice this means that it will not be possible to break at the
\c main function of the application before the \c loadApp function of the .NET
host is called. Debugging native code executed before this call can only be
done in a native-only debug session.

\section2 Changes to \b Foobar's generated code in \b VS#2

It's possible to make changes to the generated native code inside \b VS#2 and
debug those changes right away.

\list 1
\li In \b VS#2, make changes to the generated native code.

    \list
    \li These changes will, of course, not be reflected in the code generation
        rules. Rebuilding \b Foobar in \b VS#1 will overwrite any changes made
        to the generated code in \b VS#2.
    \endlist

\li Press F5.

    \list
    \li The generated \e foo_bar.sln solution is built, taking into account the
        changes made.
    \li The `foo_bar.exe` is copied to the `bin` directory.
    \li The \b Foobar app starts in debug mode.
    \endlist
\endlist

\section2 Changes to \b Foobar C# code with both \b VS#1 and \b VS#2 opened

\list 1
\li In \b VS#1, make changes to the C# code of \b Foobar.

\li Build \b Foobar in \b VS#1 in the \c Debug configuration.

    \list
    \li This will overwrite any changes made to the generated code in \b VS#2.
    \endlist

\li Switch to \b VS#2 and press F5.

    \list
    \li The \b Foobar app starts in debug mode.
    \li The changes to the C# code should be in effect.
    \endlist
\endlist

\section2 Changes to generation rules with both \b VS#1 and \b VS#2 opened

This requires a rebuild of \b Foobar because the incremental build up-to-date
check for the project will not include the generation rules (or the generator
tool).

\list 1
\li In \b VS#1, make changes to the generation rules (or the generator tool).

\li Clean the \b Foobar project.

    \list
    \li For example: right-click on \b Foobar and select \e "Clean".

    \li If \b VS#2 is opened, this will result in an error due to the output dir
        of the generated code being in use. Ignore this error.
    \endlist

\li Build the \b Foobar project in the `Debug` configuration.

    \list
    \li Assuming project dependencies are correctly set up, changes to the
        generation rules (or the generator tool) will be included in the build
        and be in effect when generating the code for the \b Foobar project.

    \li This will overwrite any changes made to the generated code in \b VS#2.
    \endlist

\li Switch to \b VS#2 and press F5.

    \list
    \li The \b Foobar app starts in debug mode.
    \li The changes to the generation rules (or the generator tool) should be in
        effect.
    \endlist
\endlist

*/
